 "notes": "All trained rows (ERM, RandMatch, MatchDG, PerfMatch) use the commands in reproduce_scripts/reproduce_rmnist_lenet.py, with one command per target domain and the other five domains as train domains. The script's train_domains string is written as separate argv tokens here. I took the data size of 1000 samples per domain (--subset_size 1000) from the paper (supplement C.1). The README only shows the ResNet-18 data generation with subset_size 2000. I used --model lenet --img_h 32 --img_w 32 to match the script's lenet setup and data/data_gen_mnist.py:214. The data directory is rot_mnist_lenet, which matches --mnist_case lenet.\n\nThe per-run number comes from train.py's per-epoch line 'Current Best Epoch: k with Test Accuracy: v' (algorithms/erm_match.py:160 and match_dg.py:386). The last such line in each 'Run n' block is the test accuracy at the best source-validation epoch. This equals the per-run value behind train.py:314 'Final Test Accuracy (Source Validation)'. I did not use that summary line because it is printed over all runs. n_runs defaults to 3 and n_runs_matchdg_erm defaults to 1, so there are 3 runs per cell. No separate test.py evaluation is needed, because train.py computes test accuracy itself.\n\nMatchDG has two phases. Phase 1 is matchdg_ctr and Phase 2 is matchdg_erm. The number is read from the Phase 2 log (step erm_phase). Both phases run all 3 seeds in turn with the same settings.\n\nThe Average columns are derived as the mean of the six target-domain cells. CCSA, D-MTAE, LabelGrad, DAN, CrossGrad and DIVA are copied from Shankar et al. 2018 and Ilse et al. 2020, so they are unlinked."
